WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:04.920
- [Pulkit] We really believe

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.590
that dexterous manipulation
is going to be the future

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.790
in robotics.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.950
What can robots do today very well,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.270
is they can move around

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.530
and they can pick up objects.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.210
But just imagine for a moment,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.500
what do you do you do in your house

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.090
or what happens in assembly?

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.330
You might pick up a screwdriver,
use it to screw in a screw.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.500
You might pick up a knife,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.870
use it to peel a vegetable or cut it.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.510
What these things involve
is picking an object,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.950
but then making sure

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.490
that the blade of the
knife faces the vegetable.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.220
This task is what we
call is reorientation.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.080
If I take an object in one pose,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.530
I try to rotate it into
a different target pose.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.470
Now, this involves my fingers

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.170
to make and break contact with the object,

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.970
which makes it quite challenging.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.150
And also, the fact that
I have my fingers move

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.920
in coordination with each other.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.000
Now, what we present today

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.120
is a versatile dexterous
manipulation system,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.850
which can reorient many complex shapes,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.130
just with one depth camera.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.020
And everything is now open-source.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.990
So it turns out, currently,
robots could pick up objects

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.630
and that is why you're seeing
a lot of deployment happening

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.823
in logistics and in warehousing.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.900
But what robots couldn't
do before was to use tools.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.910
And one of the key enablers

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.240
is for the system to reorient objects.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.960
So as you see now, this robot
is reorienting the object.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.060
It has four fingers which are moving

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.160
to get the object in the right shape.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.010
- [Tao] It performs best when
we do it on the train objects,

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.430
and it has some decent
generalization capabilities,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.180
so it can work to some extent
on some unknown objects.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.640
And of course there's a
big room for improvement.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.280
So we are still working on
improving the generalization

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.960
aspects of this controller.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.580
- [Pulkit] So what would
it take for someone

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.350
to get this up and running?

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.210
How much time do you think it'll take?

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.520
- So amount of time it
takes to build the hardware,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.200
which takes a few hours.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.050
And then the second part
is setting up the software

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.750
and we provide the docker image
so people can just download

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.610
and then run the code directory.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.470
So in the upfront software side,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.630
it should be even less than one hour. This

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.120
- Is our effort towards making robots

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.600
or robotics being more democratized,
being more open source.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.300
- We train everything in simulation and

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.880
because in simulation
we can get tons of data

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.390
and so we can really address
these challenging issues.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.700
And eventually we transfer this controller

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.950
from the simulation to the real world.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.800
We open source the code
and also pre-train model

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.270
and also the CAD model

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.640
so everyone can replicate our results.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.840
And this is very important

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.900
to promote the dexus
manipulation research.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.080
And another thing that's very
different from what we did to

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.200
what OpenAI did is we
actually in our setup

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.240
or the hand is facing downward.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.780
And in this case, the
graph really matters.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.720
- My hand is protecting against gravity.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.490
But just imagine how
do you use your knife?

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.960
How do you use screwdrivers?

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.390
Your hand is always facing on the top.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.750
So now when I'm reorienting
this object, the problem is

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.870
that if I make one small mistake,
the object can drop down.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.540
And most importantly, we don't
have to assume a fixed set

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.458
of objects, which means that
if you have a new object,

00:03:09.458 --> 00:03:12.284
if you're in a household in your factory,

00:03:12.284 --> 00:03:13.782
you get something new, you don't have

00:03:13.782 --> 00:03:16.080
to go and retrain the system.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.830
You can use our system to
hopefully still be able

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.450
to reorient the object.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.310
We can think of even more things

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.040
where the technology might
have potential applications.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.630
What about prosthetics style?

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.030
- If a robot can use
different kind of tools,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.030
then it can really help the
people with disabilities

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.670
to use those tools to do daily tasks.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.150
- One of the things which
has restricted robotics

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.520
for quite some time is if
you have a new task, you have

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.170
to build a new controller.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.470
It requires teams of people and weeks

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.720
or months, even half a year of time

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.000
to design a controller for that task.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.710
And one thing with Tao

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:54.029
and other people in the
lab we have been trying

00:03:54.029 --> 00:03:55.697
to develop is a paradigm

00:03:55.697 --> 00:03:58.980
where we can train controllers very easily

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.720
with very less human effort.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.210
The system is completely centurial,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.210
but how, you know, we know
that once things are centurial,

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.490
they end up having some issues

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.700
because simulation is
always a model of reality

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.560
and not reality per se.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.420
- So the way we do centurial is

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.030
that we train a neural network
controller in the simulation

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.839
and this simulation is a digital twin

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.900
of the real world system.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.810
And after we train the
controller, we just transfer it

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.350
to the real system and
the in a zero short way.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.810
In terms of the simulation
to the real world gap,

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:31.643
there are mainly two gaps.

00:04:31.643 --> 00:04:33.540
So one is the perception gap.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.554
The images from the camera looks different

00:04:36.554 --> 00:04:40.650
when it's in the simulation and
when it's in the real world.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.460
- And that is perhaps because
the rendering is not as good.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.620
- The rendering in the real
world is much more noisy.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.431
And in the simulation the
rendering is very clean.

00:04:49.431 --> 00:04:51.720
And another gap is dynamics.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.478
This motors behaves actually different

00:04:53.478 --> 00:04:56.475
when these real motors and
also the simulated motors.

00:04:56.475 --> 00:05:00.425
And while we get reasonable
centurial transfer performance,

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:03.000
there's still some room for improvement.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.700
- So of course we have not
solved the reorientation problem.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.390
There are many limitations
there still exist

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.575
that the system is not as
precise as we want it to be.

00:05:10.575 --> 00:05:13.433
Sometimes it also drops
objects, unfortunately.

00:05:13.433 --> 00:05:17.190
The other thing we are really
excited about is the future

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.690
of Dexter hands.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.895
You know, for a long time
people have been developing many

00:05:20.895 --> 00:05:23.056
different kinds of Dexter hands,

00:05:23.056 --> 00:05:25.773
but they did not exist in
algorithm which could perform

00:05:25.773 --> 00:05:28.763
complex manipulation
such as reorientation.

00:05:28.763 --> 00:05:32.669
But now that we have the
algorithm, we think this space

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:35.223
of Dexter's hands is
going to heat up again

00:05:35.223 --> 00:05:38.846
and which might change the
landscape of what people think

00:05:38.846 --> 00:05:42.863
robots are able to do today
versus what they can do

00:05:42.863 --> 00:05:45.840
very shortly in the future.

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.630
And that thing really is tool use, right?

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.222
Not just picking objects, but using them.

00:05:51.222 --> 00:05:53.381
The future is truly exciting.

00:05:53.381 --> 00:05:56.586
The future is going to belong
to Dexter manipulation.

00:05:56.586 --> 00:05:59.100
And what we're giving you today is just

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.750
a preview into the future.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:02.823
So stay tuned and we'll be back.

